            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Lists the positions of every non-zero (true) cell in a tensor.\n\nWhere t.count_nonzero says HOW MANY cells of a mask are set, t.argwhere says WHICH: which joints are over the speed limit, which frames are glitched, which pixels are above threshold. Each found cell comes out as its coordinates.\n\nt.non_zero IS THE SAME NODE:\nt.non_zero is another name for exactly this node - same code, same answer (it matches torch.nonzero, which gives the same coordinates).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.argwhere\nt.non_zero\nNeither takes arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nAny tensor, usually a mask from a comparison (t.gt, t.lt, ...). A cell counts as found when it is not zero - so a plain tensor of measurements finds nearly everything. Every tensor that arrives is searched and the answer sent. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor first; an empty tensor is ignored (nothing is sent).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindex tensor where non-zero:\nA whole-number (int64) tensor with one ROW per found cell and one column per dim of the input: for a table each row is (row, column), for a 1-D tensor a single position. The rows come in reading order - along the first row, then the next. Its shape is (number found, number of dims), so its length changes from one tensor to the next; when nothing is found it is empty, shape [0, 2] for a table.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1; t.gt marks those above the threshold slider (starting at 0.85, so only a few), drawn as a mask, white for true. t.argwhere lists the white cells as [row, column] pairs, and t.info shows its shape - the first number is how many were found. Check a pair against the picture: row 0 is the top, column 0 the left. Drag the threshold down and the list grows.\n\nRELATED:\nt.count_nonzero counts the same cells. t.any asks whether there are any. t.masked_select gives the VALUES at the true cells rather than their positions. t.argmax gives the position of the single largest value. np.argwhere is the NumPy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 712,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Lists the positions of every non-zero (true) cell in a tensor.\n\nWhere t.count_nonzero says HOW MANY cells of a mask are set, t.argwhere says WHICH: which joints are over the speed limit, which frames are glitched, which pixels are above threshold. Each found cell comes out as its coordinates.\n\nt.non_zero IS THE SAME NODE:\nt.non_zero is another name for exactly this node - same code, same answer (it matches torch.nonzero, which gives the same coordinates).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.argwhere\nt.non_zero\nNeither takes arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nAny tensor, usually a mask from a comparison (t.gt, t.lt, ...). A cell counts as found when it is not zero - so a plain tensor of measurements finds nearly everything. Every tensor that arrives is searched and the answer sent. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor first; an empty tensor is ignored (nothing is sent).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindex tensor where non-zero:\nA whole-number (int64) tensor with one ROW per found cell and one column per dim of the input: for a table each row is (row, column), for a 1-D tensor a single position. The rows come in reading order - along the first row, then the next. Its shape is (number found, number of dims), so its length changes from one tensor to the next; when nothing is found it is empty, shape [0, 2] for a table.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1; t.gt marks those above the threshold slider (starting at 0.85, so only a few), drawn as a mask, white for true. t.argwhere lists the white cells as [row, column] pairs, and t.info shows its shape - the first number is how many were found. Check a pair against the picture: row 0 is the top, column 0 the left. Drag the threshold down and the list grows.\n\nRELATED:\nt.count_nonzero counts the same cells. t.any asks whether there are any. t.masked_select gives the VALUES at the true cells rather than their positions. t.argmax gives the position of the single largest value. np.argwhere is the NumPy version."
        },
